"""Serialize individually authored problem assessments, linking already checked source occurrences."""
import json,pathlib
P=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
C={int(c['id'].split('-')[-1]):c for c in json.loads((P/'claims.json').read_text())}
R=[]
def p(n,title,cs,his,today,art,why,worth,extra=()):
 refs=list(dict.fromkeys([r for i in cs for r in C[i]['refs']]+list(extra)))
 R.append(dict(id=f'MP-SA14-{n}',title=title,occurrences=[dict(claim=C[i]['id'],items=C[i]['items'],cote=C[i]['cote'],scans=C[i]['scans'],lines=C[i]['lines']) for i in cs],as_he_states=his,plausible_today=today,state_of_art='Checked as of November 2026: '+art,why_open=why,worth=worth,refs=refs))

p(201,'Finite-order Picard approximation and projectivity',[202,332],'For a good henselian local base, compare the images of algebraic and completed line bundles at every finite order.','Use a noetherian henselian G-ring and a locally finitely presented moduli functor of the line-bundle data.','Artin1969 Theorems1.8/1.10 prove finite-order approximation for the specified excellent henselian settings; Stacks07QX states the henselian G-ring theorem. P32 then deduces descent of projectivity from approximation of an ample class on the closed fibre, using Stacks0D2S. This does not assert equality of algebraic and completed Picard groups.','The page gives a regular-completion criterion for good, with an uncertain arrow and an explicit algebraic henselization example; no later reason for abandonment is documented.','Yes, as an exact archival comparison with approximation: first fix the moduli functor and its finite-presentation hypothesis.')
p(202,'Stronger comparison with isolated R¹ support',[203],'A stronger Picard assertion is proposed when R¹f_*O is supported at the closed point; the completed target is ambiguous.','Separate a proper-morphism formal Picard statement from a punctured-affine statement before imposing cohomological support hypotheses.','Artin approximation and Elkik1973 III.1 give different exact theorems; neither determines the uncertain X/Y target in this sentence.','The surviving arrow is uncertain; no source states why this particular formulation was dropped.','Only after the scan resolves the target; otherwise one would research a reconstruction rather than his question.')
p(203,'Henselian punctured Picard versus completion',[204],'Does completion preserve Pic of a punctured henselian local spectrum, at least for an isolated singularity?','For a noetherian henselian pair(A,J), compare U=SpecA−V(J) with its inverse image in SpecÂ.','Elkik1973 III.1 Theorem3, Corollary1 and the following lemma/corollary on p.573 prove the map Pic(U)→Pic(U′) is bijective. The isolated-singularity restriction is unnecessary.','The page proposes resolution as a possible route; Elkik provides the later algebraization route. Her acknowledgement of Boutot is not a checked reading of his earlier work.','Useful for commentary and provenance; no new theorem is needed for this exact question.')
p(204,'Separation and representability of the numerical quotient',[206,207],'The coset construction is expected to give a separated or unramified quotient under closedness/properness hypotheses.','For a represented quotient sheaf with faithfully flat quotient map, test separatedness by the closed kernel relation; scheme existence and unramifiedness require separate hypotheses.','The diagonal criterion follows by descent; Kleiman2005 Lemma4.9 proves a specific quasi-projective smooth proper equivalence-relation quotient. It does not represent every quotient in the manuscript sentence.','The ambient Pic/Picτ notation is inconsistent on276; the page gives no complete general quotient theorem.','Yes, as a short theorem-by-theorem reconstruction after fixing the ambient coset and the exact relation.')
p(205,'Represent the Néron quotient without representing Pic',[208],'It would suffice to know Picτ flat and closed, possibly without constructing all of Pic.','Distinguish quotient algebraic spaces from quotient schemes and specify which Picard sheaf is algebraic.','Kleiman2005 Remark4.18 states modern algebraic-space representability, while Lemma4.9 gives a scheme quotient under stronger hypotheses. No opened source matches the full scheme-valued hope without representing Pic.','The margin explicitly states a sufficiency hope; no historical explanation for its later fate is supplied.','Moderate interest as a scope audit, beginning with the algebraic-space formulation rather than an unsupported scheme claim.')
p(206,'Equivariant correspondences and the Picard component',[212,264],'The quotient construction uses equivariant maps into A′; the correspondence functor is later marked yes by Raynaud.','For pointed proper families with universal O-pushforward and a represented rigidified Picard functor, isolate the correspondence factor; restrict to the appropriate connected/torsion component.','P22 verifies the rigidification formula and refutes the full-Pic interpretation. FGA236 and Kleiman §2 provide the functor framework; the exact relative correspondence theorem attributed to Raynaud was not located. SGA VI XII1.1 is a different pullback theorem.','A missing superscript and an unexpanded attribution obstruct identification, rather than constituting evidence of an unsolved theorem.','High archival value: recover the component qualifier and compare the exact Raynaud functor before doing new geometry.')
p(207,'Four desired Hodge–Picard pathologies',[214,215],'The invariant-space formula is to produce four examples, only tersely listed.','Specify each invariant, base, fibre characteristic and comparison map; distinguish h¹(O), h⁰(Ω¹), Picard dimension and tangent dimension.','FGA236 §2.9 gives nonreduced Picard schemes and a nonflat Mumford family. Oda1969 Corollaries5.11–5.12 gives the precise absolute Dieudonné/de Rham comparison. P11 shows the inversion control alone has equal Hodge dimensions.','The page announces examples without constructions; no source explains whether all four were subsequently realized in this form.','High interest once the four conditions are made exact; one run can test the simplest invariant representation, without claiming all four realized.')
p(208,'Realize a p-divisibility jump',[216,217],'Construct finite flat subgroups whose quotient homomorphism becomes divisible by p only on the special fibre.','Require [p]F⊂G for descent and F_generic not⊂G_generic but F_special⊂G_special for the divisibility jump.','P10 proves this conditional criterion. Maulik–Poonen2012 Proposition3.6 and Example3.12 establish p-primary nonsaturation of NS specialization by a different CM construction.','The literal pF=G and generic inclusion conflict with the intended effect; no completed family appears on289–290.','High interest as an explicit construction task after the subgroup arrows are settled; the phenomenon itself is established.')
p(209,'Relative divisible or reduced Picard part',[223],'Construct or lift the Picard part written Pic^{00|∞} over Artinian or reduced bases.','Distinguish a reduced identity subgroup, its maximal abelian part, its p-divisible group and pointwise lifting through square-zero thickenings.','Oda1969 Proposition4.9 and Corollaries5.11–5.12 settle precise absolute perfect-field comparisons; FGA236 §2.6 gives obstruction groups and §2.9 rejects blanket flatness. None identifies the uncertain relative functor as written.','The superscript and arrows are doubtful; the page does not explain a later abandonment.','High interest after a scan check, because confusing these four objects produces false lifting conclusions.')
p(210,'The detached Roos vanishing bound',[226,227],'An induction claims vanishing of high derived limits and questions extension beyond filtered indexing.','Retain a small index category, finite module hypotheses and a finite regular resolution; identify the missing L_k before stating a bound.','Laudal1965 §1.1 and Corollary1.3.2 give the opened derived-limit/Ext framework; P24 verifies the two spectral sequences under explicit module hypotheses. The precise Roos bound remains unidentified.','The sheet begins after the needed definition; it is reused printed material, not a newly formulated Picard problem.','Worth a source-identification run, starting from Roos1961/1962 references, not a speculative new vanishing theorem.')
p(211,'Existence of the gluing involution quotient',[229],'After pinching punctured affine planes, check the involution quotient and the proposed quasi-affine behavior.','Use an existing finite pushout and verify that every finite group orbit has a common affine neighborhood, or formulate an algebraic-space quotient.','Ferrand2003 Theorem5.4 and7.1 give precise pinching/existence criteria. P12 proves the functions are constant along the glued G_m but leaves the quotient criterion separate.','The manuscript marks the common-neighborhood step as needing verification; the global-functions calculation does not imply it.','A good bounded construction task: write the local quotient charts and check their overlap.')
p(212,'Kummer and Artin–Schreier–Witt exposition',[233],'The editorial sheet requests treatments of the named theories without a specific new assertion.','Treat this as an exposition task until the intended proposition and characteristic assumptions are recovered.','The opened field-theory and representation sources identify the surrounding classical context; no new theorem can be extracted from the two topic names.','The fragment supplies a programme, not an unsolved proposition or an account of abandonment.','Low research priority; useful only for identifying the reused editorial source.')
p(213,'Stable finite-dimensional linear system',[238],'From divisors x·D linearly equivalent to D, obtain a stable finite-dimensional system and a morphism X×P→P.','Specify a group action and a compatible projective linearization of the line bundle; properness controls dimension of sections but not the action data.','Kleiman2005 §3 treats relative divisors and linear systems under cohomology/base-change conditions. No opened result identifies the unspecified operation on306.','The operation and compatibility assumptions are absent on this face.','Potentially useful once the preceding sheet or exact action is found; not yet a well-posed new problem.')
p(214,'Finite-type pieces and quasi-compact closures',[240,241],'Are connected components or closures of Picard pieces of finite type? A later margin rejects the general connected-component assertion.','Distinguish geometric components, global connected components, Picτ and Hilbert-polynomial strata over the base.','SGA VI XII1.2 gives a decomposition into finite-type quasi-projective opens after generic shrinking. Kleiman2005 Theorem6.20 gives finite-type open-and-closed Hilbert strata for projective flat geometrically integral families. Neither proves the unrestricted closure sentence.','The manuscript records its own negative revision; its counterexample is not supplied.','Useful for commentary; a new run should identify the annotated counterexample rather than equate all component notions.')
p(215,'Universal openness of Picτ',[243],'Is Picτ→S universally open, especially on tame torsion and in characteristic0?','Keep the normal/proper, represented and smoothness hypotheses; openness of multiplication is distinct from openness over S.','FGA236 §§2.5–2.10 and3.5 prove tame étaleness and specified smoothness/variation results. These settle the indicated smooth/tame cases but not every terse clause.','The question list leaves the scope of its margins uncertain.','Moderate interest: first rewrite the exact morphism and locus, then compare the smoothness theorem.')
p(216,'Flatness of Picτ',[244,258],'The page initially asks flatness, then records a negative answer attributed to Mumford.','No unconditional flatness statement for relative Picτ; isolate hypotheses such as H²=0 or normal smooth identity components.','FGA236 §2.9 records Mumford’s nonflat family over an Artinian base; §2.10 supplies positive smoothness criteria. P11 proves the simpler Y′×A′[2] example is flat, though nonsmooth. Prop2.10(i) propagates identity flatness only to Pic^σ, the tame torsion part, and section points; it does not itself prove the extra wild implication written on311.','The author’s later margin gives the reason for rejection: a counterexample.','Worth explaining carefully; no new counterexample search is required for the exact negative statement.')
p(217,'Separatedness under weaker fibre hypotheses',[246,261,262],'Ask separation of Pic, Picτ or n-torsion under geometrically locally integral or separability-type fibres; mention a double elliptic fibre.','Name the specific subgroup and assume the appropriate geometric fibre condition; normal-proper represented Pic gives a verified positive case.','FGA236 §2.3 establishes normal-family results; Kleiman2005 Theorem4.8 proves Picard existence and separatedness for flat projective families with geometrically integral fibres, and §4.18 gives the broader representability setting. This supplies an exact positive case weaker than normality. The full merely proper locally-integral scope and the double-fibre model remain unidentified here.','The drawing and the word séparables do not specify one modern theorem.','High archival interest; reconstruct the double-fibre family before claiming a counterexample to any precise modern separation criterion.')
p(218,'Variation of Picard dimensions',[247,263],'Ask semicontinuity of dimension and of α,μ,λ, with inequalities whose primes indicate specialization.','Separate reduced-group dimension α+μ+λ, tame Tate rank2α+μ and tangent dimension h¹(O); specify which point specializes to which.','FGA236 §§2.7–2.8 pp.234–235 proves upper semicontinuity of total dimension, constancy without additive components and the stated tame-rank inequality; it expressly presents α≤α′, λ≥λ′ and blanket dimension constancy as conjectures. SGA3 II X Remark8.7 states the α/unipotent inequalities for smooth finite-type groups and calls the merely flat extension probable. The general nonsmooth manuscript version is not certified settled or currently open by this bounded source set; the prime orientation is s special, s′ generic in FGA.','The pages are a question list with partly answered margins; no reason for dropping the broader variants is documented.','Useful as a formula audit and geometric example comparison, after fixing prime orientation.')
p(219,'Local constancy of tame Tate rank',[248],'Expect the ℓ-adic Picard rank to be locally constant in the indicated normal/proper setting.','Use ℓ invertible on the base and the represented reduced identity component; do not replace the rank by NS rank.','FGA236 §§2.5–2.8 controls tame torsion and component variation; Maulik–Poonen2012 shows that NS rank behaves differently. The exact blanket relative statement remains a scope question.','The symbol and family conditions are abbreviated on the page.','Moderate priority: compare the precise Tate module, not a numerical Picard-rank slogan.')
p(220,'Hyperplane restriction on Picard objects',[250],'Ask whether a hyperplane restriction map is an isomorphism or surjective; the superscript and arrow are uncertain.','Use Lef/Leff plus the appropriate dimension, depth and conormal-cohomology bounds; specify Pic classes versus the line-bundle category.','SGA2 XI1–3, especially3.18, gives exact restriction statements. The source arrow cannot be replaced by whichever version is true.','The question is legible but its functor decoration is not.','High value for the companion commentary; scan first, then attach one exact theorem.')
p(221,'Wild multiplication on Picτ',[252],'Is multiplication by arbitrary n universally open? The negative annotation has an uncertain target.','The tame n-invertible case is étale; variants excluding additive parts need separate formulation.','FGA236 Proposition2.5 gives the tame case. In characteristic p, [p]:G_a→G_a is constant and not open, disproving a broad assertion that permits an additive component.','The source’s negative margin may refer to this or a neighboring clause; no attachment is imposed.','Useful as a bounded group-scheme test; no broad wild theorem should be inferred.')
p(222,'Finite type of nonzero multiplication',[253],'Ask whether multiplication by n on Pic is of finite type.','Assume n≠0, X proper over a noetherian base; use the fppf Picard functor even before global scheme representability.','SGA VI XIII Theorem3.6 p.635 proves precisely this general result; FGA’s complement(iv) announced it in1962.','The manuscript leaves a question; the published finiteness theory settles it.','Commentary rather than new research; retain the essential n≠0 restriction.')
p(223,'Projectivity of Picτ for normal families',[254],'The smooth projective result is queried, and normality is proposed as a possible weakening.','State the smooth projective geometrically integral theorem separately from normal/proper projectivity over an arbitrary base.','FGA236 Corollary4.2 and Kleiman2005 Exercise/Answer6.18 give the smooth projective case. Normal-family properness alone does not produce an ample relative line bundle.','The page does not provide the extra polarization argument.','Moderate interest, starting with the exact base and polarization assumptions rather than repeating properness.')
p(224,'Finitely many torsion sections',[255],'There should be finitely many S-points of fixed torsion order; the author answers no.','Finite torsion group schemes need not have finite S-valued point sets over nonreduced bases.','P26 gives an exact counterexample for every positive-dimensional abelian variety over an infinite characteristic-p field, using first-order sections and d[p]=0. FGA236 and Oda give the surrounding nonreduced torsion theory.','The negative answer is written on the page; its particular example is not specified.','Low research priority, high explanatory value because it prevents a recurrent points-versus-schemes error.')
p(225,'Variation and boundedness of NS torsion',[256,259],'Ask continuity of NS torsion, suggesting an Igusa modular family; elsewhere mark bounded rank yes.','Distinguish uniform boundedness, constructibility, local constancy and p-primary specialization defects.','SGA VI XIII5.1 p.650 proves uniform rank and torsion-order bounds. Maulik–Poonen2012 Proposition3.6 gives tame saturation control and Example3.12 an actual p-primary defect. The proposed modular family is not specified enough to check.','The page proposes a test rather than a theorem or a completed example.','High interest for a focused family calculation; known boundedness does not answer its finer variation question.')
p(226,'Multiplicity under specialization',[257],'Ask compatibility with specialization and multiplicity of Picτ, without defining the invariant.','Choose scheme length, component multiplicity, tangent dimension or NS saturation explicitly before asking compatibility.','FGA236 controls dimension and smoothness; Maulik–Poonen controls geometric NS specialization. No opened source can identify the undefined multiplicity map.','The triple question marks and missing morphism are the only explanation on the page.','Only after source clarification; otherwise there is no determinate theorem to develop.')
p(227,'Closedness of Picτ without projectivity',[267],'Remove projectivity from the closedness argument; in the normal case seek proper Pic⁰.','Retain normal geometric fibres and represented Pic for the verified normal theorem; the general proper geometrically integral case is a distinct question.','FGA236 §2.3 settles the normal represented case. SGA VI XIII Remark4.8 p.650 explicitly leaves the nonprojective generalization open in that source; Kleiman2005 Remark6.19 states general openness/finite type but not the missing closedness. No2026 open-status conclusion is inferred from this bounded search.','The manuscript itself asks to remove a hypothesis. The later source documents that this weakening was not automatic.','High value as a targeted literature-update task; first isolate the exact generalization and modern algebraic-space conventions.')
p(228,'Extending algebraically trivial line bundles over a trait',[268],'Extend a generically algebraically trivial bundle over a trait and retain algebraic triviality on the special fibre.','For a proper flat geometrically normal family with represented Pic⁰ and rigidification, use its valuative properness; arbitrary line-bundle extension is separate.','FGA236 §2.3 and Kleiman2005 Theorem5.4/Proposition5.6 give the properness/extension route under their precise assumptions. The crossed Raynaud attribution is not retained as a live theorem.','The page proposes the valuative step; it does not supply a global extension proof without representability.','Useful for a precise valuative proof in the companion, with both extension and special-fibre membership stated.')
p(229,'Picard representability beyond projectivity',[269],'Does a proper flat family with normal or integral geometric fibres have a Picard scheme?','Separate field-valued representability, generic scheme representability, and global algebraic-space representability over a base.','SGA VI XII1.5(a) proves representability for every proper scheme over a field; XII1.2 gives scheme representability over a nonempty open of an integral noetherian base. Kleiman2005 §4.18 states the algebraic-space result and limitations of scheme representability.','The final page leaves the base-sensitive question open; field and relative answers differ.','High explanatory priority; no single yes/no should replace the three exact formulations.')

p(230,'Local-to-global connectedness and completion',[272,275],'Infer connectedness in dimension h from component dimensions and punctured-local connectedness; cut by n equations with loss at most n.','Require the exact completion-sensitive local hypotheses and global connectedness, with the written dimensions measured in the specified spaces.','SGA2 connectedness theory is the established framework; P1 disproves the neighboring nongeneric-geometric specialization statement, and the saved cutting_dimension calculation checks the numerical K=n+h substitution only.','Margins explicitly strengthen the local assumptions; the page is a working proof, not a final global theorem with omitted hypotheses.','Useful after merging the earlier58:2–19 occurrences with the root register; the arithmetic check is not the connectedness proof.')
p(231,'Specialization of connectedness and equidimensionality',[273,274],'A proper dominant family over a normal base should transfer the stated generic connectedness dimension; formal equidimensionality is marked doubtful.','At minimum require geometric connectedness rather than mere connectedness of the generic fibre; formal equidimensionality needs its own hypotheses.','P1 supplies a complete counterexample over Z_(5). SGA2’s connectedness theorems use completed local rings and the appropriate dimension conditions; they do not validate the literal generic-fibre assertion.','The manuscript flags the formal-equidimensional step; the counterexample identifies a separate missing geometric condition.','High priority for repairing the commentary, not for treating the false literal statement as an open conjecture.')
p(232,'Connected inverse images of hyperplanes',[276,277],'Extend the hyperplane connectedness result to inverse images under an arbitrary map to projective space.','Control fibre dimensions and connectedness as well as the number of hyperplane equations; an arbitrary map can have disconnected inverse images.','SGA2’s projective connectedness and Lefschetz theorems give exact ample-section cases. The general-morphism extension is not matched with all its missing hypotheses by the opened sources.','The face is explicitly a programme entry, without a complete statement.','Moderate research potential after recovering the intended relative-dimension bound.')
p(233,'Local hyperplane analogue',[278],'Seek a local version of the preceding connectedness statement.','Work with a completed noetherian local ring and the punctured spectrum, retaining the component/depth assumptions.','SGA2 XIII and Raynaud1974 I provide the local formal-comparison framework; no unqualified local analogue follows merely by changing projective to local.','The page states the hope without repeating the preceding hypotheses.','Useful for merging exact local occurrences, with completion retained.')
p(234,'Nonproper formal comparison programme',[279],'Advertise comparison and formal existence in a depth-minus-two range, then list a nine-part programme.','Separate comparison of cohomology, full faithfulness, algebraization, finite covers and projective-scheme effectivity; each needs its own range.','SGA2 IX–XIII and Raynaud1974 I give substantive later theorems. The repeatedly revised introductory formula is not one theorem whose every quantifier can be read back from them.','The introduction is a working programme with cancellations and insertions; no external reason for abandonment is known.','High synthesis value, but a research run should select one exact statement rather than claim to solve the whole programme.')
p(235,'Weak Lefschetz implies the next injective degree',[280],'From the weak comparison condition deduce the full LG condition, apparently through general degree n.','At n=0, use vector-bundle extensions to prove H¹ injectivity; higher degrees require an exact-category or resolution argument compatible with vector bundles.','P20 independently proves n=0. SGA2 X supplies established Lefschetz formulations, but an injective resolution in all sheaves does not prove the manuscript’s unrestricted vector-bundle argument.','The source moves quickly across the dimension-shifting step; no complete higher-degree proof was located in the cited passage.','A good short proof task: formulate the exact category and test the first nontrivial higher degree.')
p(236,'Reduction to a generating family',[281,282],'Check LG on ample/generating bundles and propagate it by the displayed short exact sequences.','Require each bundle to embed into a finite sum of tested generators with locally free cokernel, and retain both adjacent cohomological degrees.','P20 checks the long-exact-sequence argument; SGA2 X is the published context. No arbitrary-scheme resolution property is assumed.','The page’s hypotheses supply the intended generator reduction, but some endpoint indices are easily lost in modernization.','Useful as a proof check; the first task is to state the embedding hypothesis exactly.')
p(237,'Stability under neighborhoods',[283],'An unrestricted neighborhood assertion is cancelled and replaced by a qualified one.','Use the quasi-projective/generating-family version and the colimit over neighborhoods where required.','SGA2 X2 and the formal Lef/Leff definitions distinguish germ and global categories; P20 checks the relevant mechanism.','The manuscript itself withdraws the unqualified draft.','Commentary priority: preserve the correction rather than list both drafts as independent conjectures.')
p(238,'Does Lefschetz force depth two?',[284,285],'Ask prof≥2 off Y; prove only exclusion of associated points whose closure misses Y.','Retain that closure-sensitive necessary condition. A depth-two converse must specify which points and which comparison category.','P28 proves exactly the associated-point exclusion. SGA2 X and Raynaud I.1.4 give sufficient local-depth conditions, not an unrestricted converse.','The source question mark explicitly distinguishes the stronger conjecture from its preceding argument.','High interest for a counterexample or sharp converse run after the locus is fixed.')
p(239,'Transport algebra structures and algebraize étale covers',[286,287],'Lift finite algebra structures and obtain finite étale covers from the formal neighborhood.','Use full faithfulness on tensor powers plus effectivity of the finite locally free module, then shrink to remove the nonétale locus.','SGA2 X2.3–2.6 gives the published finite-cover applications; P20 verifies the tensor-algebra mechanism.','The page distinguishes ordinary LG from effective LG; dropping that distinction leaves the existence step open.','Useful for a detailed proof appendix; it is established under the stated hypotheses.')
p(240,'From the neighborhood π₁ to the global π₁',[289],'Identify π₁(Y) with a limit over neighborhoods and ask for the stronger global consequence.','Keep the inverse limit over U⊃Y; extending a finite étale cover over X−U needs a separate purity theorem.','SGA2 X2.6 gives the neighborhood statement. Suzuki2023 Theorem1.2 proves finite generation only for the maximal pro-nilpotent local quotient under its stated complete-local assumptions; it does not remove this purity step.','The source limit is explicit; any global replacement is an extra inference.','High value as a scope audit, particularly when connecting with the earlier local fundamental-group questions.',extra=['Suzuki2023FundamentalGroups'])
p(241,'Picard classes versus the line-bundle category',[290,291,292],'Use H¹/H² of conormal powers to obtain Picard comparison, then state a categorical full-faithfulness/equivalence.','Use H¹ for uniqueness of classes and H² for lifting; add H⁰ unit control to obtain the categorical assertion.','SGA2 XI2.1–2.2 supplies the group-level criteria. P2 refutes the categorical inference for Spec k[[t]]→Spec k by automorphisms1 and1+t.','The page conflates isomorphism classes and morphisms; no later explanation is supplied.','High explanatory priority; the correction is exact and the counterexample satisfies every written positive-cohomology hypothesis.')
p(242,'Cohomological permanence under projective morphisms',[293],'Transfer the base comparison range to a coherent sheaf flat over the base with proper support; reorganize the cancelled first theorem.','Require a bounded locally free model of Rf_*F, compatible derived base change and formal limits, under noetherian quasi-projective/projective hypotheses.','P21 checks this conditional modern proof route. SGA2 formal existence gives related concrete cases but was not found to state every quantifier of the broad permanence theorem.','The page replaces its first draft and changes the flatness wording; the base X/Y reading remains important.','High potential for a bounded proof task: finish the perfect-complex argument with its exact range before touching effectivity.')
p(243,'Effectivity for relatively flat coherent sheaves',[294],'Algebraize a coherent formal sheaf with proper support, possibly after shrinking the base around Y.','Separate existence of a sheaf from comparison of its Hom and cohomology; retain flatness, proper support and an explicit algebraization theorem.','SGA2 IX2.2 and XII3.1–3.2 prove precise local/projective algebraization statements. P21 does not prove the broader manuscript effectivity assertion, whose exact match remains unidentified.','The source marks effectivity as an extra task, not a consequence already established by cohomological comparison.','High research interest after a statement audit; the first step is to test a polarized projective model satisfying SGA2 XII3.1.')
p(244,'Permanence for projective flat schemes',[295],'Extend the coherent-module theorem to projective flat schemes over the base.','Algebraize a relatively ample line bundle and its graded algebra, preserving finite presentation and flatness, before reconstructing the projective scheme.','SGA2 XII’s formal-existence theorems supply bounded ingredients. The broad corollary requires additional polarized-algebra descent not proved by P21.','The page’s margin expresses uncertainty about this categorical refinement.','Moderate-to-high interest; a run should keep the chosen polarization in the moduli problem.')
p(245,'Auxiliary faithfully flat coherent module',[296],'Use a faithfully flat coherent module to extend the permanence statement.','Specify the module’s base, finite-presentation status and the descent data it is meant to detect.','The opened formal-existence sources do not identify this terse margin as one complete theorem; it remains a formulation problem.','The auxiliary object is not defined sufficiently on the page.','Low priority until the margin is read with its full surrounding diagram.')
p(246,'Removing quasi-projectivity',[298],'Hope that quasi-projectivity can be omitted, with doubt for the projective-scheme version.','Replace the ample-family/resolution argument by a theorem that actually applies to the intended arbitrary base.','SGA2 and Raynaud provide powerful formal comparison, but no opened source matches the full weakened permanence/effectivity package. This is bounded non-identification, not a claim of current openness.','The author explicitly presents the omission as a hope.','Worth a focused literature search after fixing whether the target is coherent sheaves, algebras or polarized schemes.')
p(247,'Necessity of flatness and proper support',[299,297],'Say the hypotheses are essential, request examples, and cancel a proposal to omit properness.','Test each hypothesis independently in a fully specified permanence or algebraization assertion.','P21 proves why proper support makes the nonflat/nonfree locus removable by shrinking. SGA2 XII retains the necessary finite/coherent inputs; no actual counterexamples to every missing hypothesis are supplied here.','The page itself requests examples rather than giving them.','A good bounded examples task; begin with failure of closed-image control when support is not proper.')
p(248,'Fullness of the completed Serre quotient',[300,306],'Completion should induce a full functor from coherent sheaves modulo supports missing D.','The exact quotient functor is faithful/conservative; fullness needs depth or bounded-pole restrictions.','P29 proves exactness and the kernel description. P7 refutes unrestricted fullness using B_(x)⊂k((z))[[x]] and the series with pole orders n²; Raynaud1974 supplies a positive depth-controlled Hom theorem.','The manuscript strengthens a valid kernel observation into an unverified full-faithfulness claim.','High value for clarifying the exact category; the unqualified version is already refuted.')
p(249,'Target-only depth in formal Hom comparison',[302],'The Hom comparison should depend on the target’s depth, not a matching source-depth bound.','A is noetherian local, quotient of a regular ring, complete for(t); P is its puncture and D=V(t)∩P. Require depthG≥2 at points closed in P outside D.','Raynaud1974 I.1.1/I.1.3 yields the exact isomorphism Hom_P(F,G)≅Hom_formal(F̂,Ĝ) after the closure-dimension reduction proved in P6; P5 proves the local Hom-depth inequality.','The source has heavy revisions, but this clean substatement is identified and settled.','High commentary value; no novelty claim remains for this precise L58 formulation.')
p(250,'t-regular reduction and low-dimensional cycles',[303,304],'Kill t-power torsion, then impose revised depth and associated-cycle restrictions to get comparison.','Noetherian stabilization makes t regular on the quotient, but comparison still needs exact off-divisor depth and closure-dimension conditions.','P29 proves the torsion reduction. Raynaud I.1.1–1.4 provides precise comparison hypotheses; the manuscript’s erased/inserted dimensions remain a scan problem.','The final depth bound is blank or revised; t-regularity alone was not claimed to fill it.','Useful after ink review; the first task is to measure every closure dimension in the correct punctured space.')
p(251,'Essential image and coherent hull',[305,307],'Describe the image of completion by conditions a,b,c′ and T₁, constructing a coherent direct-image hull.','Require an explicit extension to the completed affine scheme and a verified boundary coherence criterion; undefined labels cannot be supplied by analogy.','SGA2 IX/XII and Raynaud I give exact existence/comparison theorems; P7 shows why arbitrary formal modules cannot be assumed to arise from the quotient. The full source corollary is not identifiable.','The referenced conditions are missing from the supplied faces.','High archival but limited immediate mathematical value: recover definitions before proving an image theorem.')
p(252,'The reused formal-existence theorem',[308],'The printed theorem/corollary asserts algebraization in a projective relative ample-divisor setting; a depth inequality is partly missing.','Use precisely SGA2 XII3.1–3.2 with its fibrewise regularity, flatness and depth conditions.','The two reused faces match those published statements. Their independent handwritten graded additions are not duplicates and are checked separately.','This is an earlier printed theorem reused as paper; the missing typewriter sign is a reading issue rather than a new unresolved theorem.','Source identification is useful, but there is no new research problem once the exact printed theorem is attached.')
p(253,'Negative twists, duality, and the α direction',[309,310],'Analyze negative-twist vanishing by localization and duality; the text writes a surjectivity condition for α.','Exactness requires injectivity of α when the preceding restriction is surjective; retain the dual degree r−i and projective ambient hypotheses.','P4 proves the direction and P30 checks the duality route. SGA2 XII§1,§5 gives the published projective criteria.','The sign/direction error is internal to the displayed exact sequence, but the ink is undecided.','High scan priority and low remaining proof cost; no broad equivalence should survive with the wrong direction.')
p(254,'Positive graded finiteness versus negative vanishing',[311],'Claim an equivalence between finite generation in positive twists and eventual negative-twist vanishing.','Treat them as separate properties and specify cohomological degree; the literal degree0 assertion is false.','P3 and the saved calculation give a skyscraper counterexample. SGA2 XII5.2–5.6 keeps the relevant ranges separate.','The page’s chain combines different questions; no authorial later correction is visible here.','Useful for a corrected theorem table; the false equivalence itself needs no further search.')
p(255,'The inverse-limit interchange',[312],'Replace formal cohomology by the inverse limit of cohomology on thickenings.','Control the lim¹ obstruction, for example by Mittag–Leffler under the exact coherent-finiteness assumptions.','SGA2 IX2.1 provides a uniform ML conclusion in its adjacent-degree finite setting; Laudal1965 gives derived-limit machinery. P30 records the exact obstruction sequence.','The page leaves the limiting step compressed or flagged; it is not formal for arbitrary systems.','A useful proof-completion task: identify the specific system and prove ML, without adding a gratuitous properness assumption.')
p(256,'Scope of the starred depth condition',[313,314],'Use condition(*) near the missing boundary to infer algebraic/formal comparison; later warn sufficient conditions need not be necessary.','Specify whether(*) constrains interior points away from the divisor as well as the boundary; preserve the one-way sufficient implication.','P3 refutes the boundary-only reading when U=X and a skyscraper lies outside Y. Raynaud1974 gives exact sufficient depth criteria; neither chooses the ink interpretation.','The star’s referent is unclear; the source itself cautions against a converse.','Highest scan priority because the two readings distinguish a theorem from a counterexample.')
p(257,'Twist-sign consistency',[315],'The final restriction map changes m to −m relative to its preceding argument.','Use a consistent twist on both sides of restriction and distinguish positive and negative asymptotics.','SGA2 XII§§1,5 provides the separately ranged results; P3 gives a degree0 test showing why the sign matters.','Only the scan can determine whether the minus is present or cancelled.','A scan task, not a new mathematical direction until the sign is fixed.')
p(258,'Coherence and the degree-zero hull',[316,317],'Relate positive graded cohomology, higher direct-image coherence/vanishing and depth.','For i>0 use R^i j_*≈H_Z^{i+1}; in degree0 retain F=j_*F|U or the separate H_Z⁰/H_Z¹ conditions.','SGA2 XII5.2–5.6 gives the exact finiteness/vanishing statements. P30 checks the localization endpoint and why the hull equality is structural.','The page explicitly writes the hull condition; modernization risks suppressing it.','Useful for a precise four-question chart; the first step is to retain degree0 as its own row.')
p(259,'Which cone has Picard group zero?',[323,324],'Several primed/tilde cones are boxed with Pic=0 questions; one is written SpecS[z] and another called projective.','Specify the affine cone, punctured cone, total line-bundle space and projective cone separately; for a reduced seminormal finite-type graded ring with R₀=k, affine Pic is zero.','EGA II§8 supplies the exact cone models. Traverso1970 Theorem3.6 plus the grading contraction proves the stated seminormal affine case in P17; it does not identify the undefined boxes.','The missing object definitions are decisive; mathematical convenience cannot replace Spec by Proj.','High archival value; the first step is an object-and-map diagram from the scans.')
p(260,'Factorial versus parafactorial',[326],'Assert factorial⇒parafactorial in the categorical setting.','Include depth≥2 and punctured Pic=0; a one-dimensional factorial local ring is a counterexample to the unqualified categorical assertion.','SGA2 XI3.4–3.5 gives the exact criterion. P16 uses a DVR: A*→K* is not surjective although both Pic groups vanish.','The page omits a threshold or shifts between classes and categories.','High explanatory value, settled by the exact criterion rather than a new conjecture.')
p(261,'Normalization defects and genus',[327,328],'Use g=1+Σg_i+Σn_s−ν; identify a multiplicative gluing contribution.','For a reduced connected proper curve over an algebraically closed field, n_s must be δ_s, the length of the normalization quotient. For nodal curves the extra genus and torus rank are b₁ of the dual graph.','P14–P15 rederive the additive and multiplicative exact sequences; Ferrand2003 supplies pinching descent. A branch count is insufficient for a cusp, and xy=0 in three variables is a surface.','The notation n_s and ambient dimension are not fully specified by the drawing.','Useful after merging the root’s earlier curve-normalization loci; no general tower theorem follows from the finite graph calculation.')
p(262,'The projective-cone Picard calculation',[329,330],'Correct Pic(X′)=Pic(X) to Pic(X)⊕Z and conclude Pic(Ĉ)=Z using EGA cone contraction.','Use the full section ring, Γ(X,O)=k, connected proper X, and a contraction f with f_*O=O; identify the hyperplane class and exceptional section.','EGA II8.7.5 p.176 and8.8.6 p.181 were read and visually checked; the latter gives the direct-image identity under O_Y≅f_*O_X. P18 proves the conditional Picard calculation.','The source cancels the missing-Z formula itself; other cone labels remain uncertain.','A good companion proof, contingent on identifying the model rather than silently changing the earlier Spec.')
p(263,'Picard invariance of a line-bundle total space',[331],'Pullback Pic(X)→Pic(E) is injective by the zero section; is it surjective?','For reduced seminormal finite-type X and E the total space of a line bundle, it is an isomorphism; seminormality is a sufficient added hypothesis.','Traverso1970 Theorem3.6 gives affine polynomial invariance, and P17 gives the descent argument using constant polynomial units on reduced rings. An affine cusp alone is not a counterexample to the source’s projective/full-section-ring setup.','The author writes surjectif(?), without the modern sufficient hypothesis.','High potential for a carefully scoped example task: test the actual projective X and bundle, not a different affine example.')
p(264,'The cut-off R_p group-algebra theorem',[322],'For G satisfying the undefined(R_p), Q_ℓ[G] should be decomposed when ℓ≠p.','With Fontaine’s explicit R_p condition—normal p-subgroup and cyclic prime-to-p quotient—the statement is established.','Fontaine1971 §6 Corollary1 p.168, §7 Theorems5/5′ and §8.2 Corollary1 p.179 supply the decomposition chain. Brauer1951 p.250 gives hyperelementary Schur-index detection. The manuscript’s missing definition is not restored as an ink fact.','The reused printed page stops mid-proof and lacks its earlier definition.','Low Picard priority; useful for identifying the paper stratum and avoiding a false Dieudonné connection.')

